{
  "id": "sort-colors",
  "questionId": "sort-colors",
  "slug": "sort-colors",
  "title": "Sort Colors",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Two pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "two pointers",
    "dutch national flag"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "counting each value first works, but it takes two full passes over the array. can you do it while still deciding where each element goes, in one pass?",
    "what if you tracked three regions as you scan: 'known 0s' at the front, 'known 2s' at the back, and 'unprocessed' in the middle?",
    "keep a low pointer (boundary of 0s), a high pointer (boundary of 2s), and a current pointer scanning between them",
    "when current sees a 0, swap it to low and advance both; when it sees a 2, swap it to high and shrink high (but don't advance current, since the swapped-in value still needs checking); when it sees a 1, just advance current"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "advancing the current pointer after swapping with high, which skips checking the newly swapped-in value",
    "using a general sort or a counting pass instead of the three-pointer one-pass technique",
    "mixing up which pointer moves on which value, causing 0s and 2s to end up in the wrong regions"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array containing only the values 0, 1, and 2 (think of\nthem as three colors).\n\n**Return the array sorted so all the 0s come first, then all the 1s, then all the 2s, using only a single pass over the array.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "n == nums.length",
      "1 <= n <= 300",
      "nums[i] is 0, 1, or 2"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "all elements are the same value",
      "already sorted",
      "reverse sorted (all 2s, then 1s, then 0s)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
